# Copy this file to .env. The CLI loads a working directory .env at startup, so you do not
# need to source it. A value already exported in the shell wins over the file. Every setting
# line is an optional override. Defaults are shown where they exist. Values without defaults
# use `...` as the placeholder. Uncomment only what you actually change. The default
# `--executor auto` runs a keyless Anthropic seat on your Claude Code
# subscription, so no provider key is required. Set a key below only to call the provider API.

# Provider keys are read by each SDK. Set the key for your provider to call its API rather
# than the subscription. Leave both unset to run the default keyless Anthropic seat.
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY is used with --provider anthropic, the default.
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=...
# OPENAI_API_KEY is used with --provider openai.
# OPENAI_API_KEY=...

# The primary model backend defaults to provider anthropic and model claude-opus-4-8.
# Valid providers are anthropic, openai, and litellm.
# CYBERJURY_PROVIDER=anthropic
# CYBERJURY_MODEL=claude-opus-4-8
# CYBERJURY_API_KEY overrides the SDK key above.
# CYBERJURY_API_KEY=...
# CYBERJURY_API_BASE sets a gateway or proxy endpoint for the selected provider.
# CYBERJURY_API_BASE=...
# CYBERJURY_WIRE_API selects chat or responses. Use responses for OpenAI reasoning models.
# CYBERJURY_WIRE_API=chat

# Model roles work in both review paths. The finder finds, the challenger refutes, and the
# judge confirms before a deletion. Unset role fields inherit the base backend above, so a
# single model run sets none of these and you override only the seat you want to change.
#
# Each role takes the same five fields. This block shows finder. Use the same suffixes for
# CHALLENGER_ and JUDGE_.
# Provider, model, and wire API inherit when unset. API key and base inherit only when the
# provider matches the base provider.
# CYBERJURY_FINDER_PROVIDER=...
# CYBERJURY_FINDER_MODEL=...
# CYBERJURY_FINDER_API_KEY=...
# CYBERJURY_FINDER_API_BASE=...
# CYBERJURY_FINDER_WIRE_API=...
#
# This example keeps Claude as the base finder and judge, and overrides only the GPT challenger.
# The challenger uses a different vendor than base, so it needs its own key.
# The judge differs from the challenger, so a deletion can be confirmed.
# CYBERJURY_CHALLENGER_PROVIDER=openai
# CYBERJURY_CHALLENGER_MODEL=...
# CYBERJURY_CHALLENGER_API_KEY=...
# CYBERJURY_CHALLENGER_WIRE_API=responses
# Without a distinct judge, no challenger refutation is accepted, the recall-safe default.
# `review repository --run` finds with one model, the finder.
# A seat that runs on the subscription ignores that seat's backend fields. Under
# `--executor auto`, this is usually a keyless Anthropic finder or challenger seat.

# Request behavior uses these optional overrides with defaults shown.
# CYBERJURY_RETRIES is the retry count after a failed model call.
# CYBERJURY_RETRIES=2
# CYBERJURY_TIMEOUT is the per call deadline in seconds and the retry hard cap.
# CYBERJURY_TIMEOUT=240

# The subscription executor shells out to Claude Code. The repository path gets read-only
# file tools, and the diff path gets no file tools.
# Set CYBERJURY_CLAUDE_BIN when the Claude Code binary is not on PATH.
# CYBERJURY_CLAUDE_BIN=claude
# CYBERJURY_CLAUDE_ARGS appends extra args to the claude invocation. It does not widen tools.
# CYBERJURY_CLAUDE_ARGS=
# CYBERJURY_CLAUDE_UNSAFE_TOOLS drops the read-only tool restriction. Leave it unset unless
# you trust the target.
# CYBERJURY_CLAUDE_UNSAFE_TOOLS=1

# The keyless Anthropic seat uses this optional transport setting. The default is `process`,
# one `claude -p` per model call. The `sdk` value reuses a pooled session.
# CYBERJURY_CLAUDE_TRANSPORT=process
# CYBERJURY_CLAUDE_SDK_POOL_SIZE controls sessions kept warm when transport is `sdk`.
# CYBERJURY_CLAUDE_SDK_POOL_SIZE=6
# CYBERJURY_CLAUDE_SDK_MAX_TURNS controls max turns per SDK call.
# CYBERJURY_CLAUDE_SDK_MAX_TURNS=8

# The evm domain uses this optional source fetch key when source is not already on disk.
# One key covers every chain, or pass --api-key.
# CYBERJURY_ETHERSCAN_API_KEY=...
